#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179477 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179650 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ebb0209-904f-598a-9ff2-ab9d4d0cabca" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Range of a Linear Function"}] :question/id #uuid "dc5cefa9-aa18-52d3-bbdc-a0c1050f6f3b" :question/math-academy-id "e-14168" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x) = 2x-1$ for $x \\in (-1,1]$. What is its range?" :question/worked-solution "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/32/32925e498e936298ada23ba1c560b992b7dc2dce0e39fd6479bf6ba5ce2cb545.png)\n\nThen, we find the smallest and largest $y$-values on the graph.\n\n- The smallest $y$-value is $-3$. This occurs at the point $(-1,-3)$. However, the graph does not include this point.\n- The largest $y$-value is $1$. This occurs at the point $(1,1)$.\n\nThe function covers all the $y$-values between $-3$ and $1$, including $1$ but not including $-3$.\n\nTherefore, the range is\n\n$$\n-3 < y \\leq 1\n$$\n\nwhich can also be written as\n\n$$\n-3 < f(x) \\leq 1\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179478 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179651 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6df7104d-0f74-5301-9525-132c74c19ca9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Range of a Function by Considering Its End Behavior"}] :question/id #uuid "c639f820-2493-5267-9f59-9bb06e58dea7" :question/math-academy-id "e-14171" :question/problem "What is the range of the function $f(x)$, shown below?\n\n![](images/88/880925c00705275ef8630444a08053bc6ca2c79b2e4754a09d0bd7a2af059f32.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the smallest and largest $y$-values in the graph.\n\n- The smallest $y$-value is $-1$. This occurs when $x\\to \\infty$. However, note that this point is not included in the graph.\n\n- There is no largest $y$-value.\n\nThe function covers all the $y$-values between $-1$ and $\\infty$, but not including $-1$.\n\nTherefore, the range is $-1 \\lt y \\lt \\infty$, which can also be written as $-1 \\lt f(x) \\lt \\infty$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179479 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179652 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d6727b20-2af5-5bff-aae4-e35c44fde144" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Range of a Piecewise Function"}] :question/id #uuid "1d74961e-433c-5c0f-8bdb-d279c5170cc6" :question/math-academy-id "e-14173" :question/problem "![](images/a2/a277f71617ce2dceae7ee97017655c4c27e57a9bb22020995905760e53bf7720.png)\n\nWhat is the range of the function $f(x)$, shown above?" :question/worked-solution "We find the ranges of the individual pieces, and then calculate their union:\n\n- The range of the branch defined on $x\\in [-2,1]$ is $[-2,2]$.\n- The range of the branch defined on $x\\in (1,5)$ is $(1,3]$.\n\nTherefore, the range of $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\n[-2,2] \\cup (1,3] = [-2,3]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489226 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179652 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d6727b20-2af5-5bff-aae4-e35c44fde144" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Range of a Piecewise Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "090f6b0d-954f-59b1-8627-6b16913c1f7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-3,-1]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488493} {:answer/id #uuid "63672e31-cac6-5da3-a745-b0ce239264df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,-1]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488661} {:answer/id #uuid "b22f6f36-3dde-51a4-a64b-073f992e2909" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488825} {:answer/id #uuid "f9e00e11-d23c-50d4-bca3-793cebab7b6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-3,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488997}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "090f6b0d-954f-59b1-8627-6b16913c1f7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-3,-1]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488493} :answer-field/id #uuid "0475e577-c65b-5fdd-847b-25cee8140b7d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489019} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08adecb1-4403-5fdc-8e39-bb578980f9da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[0,2]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488490} {:answer/id #uuid "2366f9ed-b709-5784-94ca-5ee3092847ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,2]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488542} {:answer/id #uuid "29a489e0-2840-50f4-8857-274000e0aaf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488558} {:answer/id #uuid "e22ff236-8516-5714-bd91-f25a3da5db1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[0,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488940}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e22ff236-8516-5714-bd91-f25a3da5db1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[0,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488940} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3a27fb8-358f-526c-99e2-e939fb691eef" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489126} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9574df1c-44a5-59df-974a-c34c608e896d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cap " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488771} {:answer/id #uuid "f54c79d8-5b80-5135-8fbf-2f4df986e61d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cup " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488990}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f54c79d8-5b80-5135-8fbf-2f4df986e61d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cup " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488990} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9d5cf43-46d1-5177-8e2d-2002b8fcbaa2" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489165}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "62541dad-de47-58c3-a80c-47be806ee721" :question/math-academy-id "q-235959" :question/problem "![](images/2d/2d46569e841a4bd7cf6646788f7bb5aec88a2386f96b8614ffec3e9c055bc200.png)\n\nWhat is the range of the function $f(x),$ shown above?\n\n$\\,$  The range is $f(x)\\in $\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "We find the ranges of the individual pieces, and then calculate their union:\n\n- The range of the branch defined on $x\\in [-3,0)$ is $[0,2)$\n\n- The range of the branch defined on $x\\in [0,3]$ is $[-3,-1]$\n\nTherefore, the range of $f(x)$ is $[-3,-1]\\cup [0,2).$\n\n**Note:** From the graph, we see that the value $y=2$ is NOT a valid output of the function $y=f(x).$ Therefore, we use parentheses $(…)$ instead of brackets $[…]$ at this endpoint."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490228 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179651 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6df7104d-0f74-5301-9525-132c74c19ca9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Range of a Function by Considering Its End Behavior"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3e2fe1e-5ce8-5259-883a-e27ba55b0ebc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489908}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3e2fe1e-5ce8-5259-883a-e27ba55b0ebc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489908} :answer-field/id #uuid "2356dd8e-95af-53aa-99c1-588dd0a4fe73" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490089} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42a84cca-e35f-5c4a-b07e-845a7d28433f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489667}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42a84cca-e35f-5c4a-b07e-845a7d28433f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489667} :answer-field/id #uuid "3443567b-7d91-5f03-9d4e-6b2b217a9066" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490103} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80e903e5-f2b3-5ed4-a09b-70f4dda6a048" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489807}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80e903e5-f2b3-5ed4-a09b-70f4dda6a048" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489807} :answer-field/id #uuid "9abeb5f8-371d-515e-a1f6-7daf65af6f1e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490163}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2c426db9-03a0-51ed-9522-bdc02ff647af" :question/math-academy-id "q-236388" :question/problem "![](images/49/49288e2c082854cfc27a2d48af2d82a96016a3c1394066471d49873e4c75878e.png)\n\nWhat is the range of the function $f(x),$ shown above?\n\nThe range is \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\n$f(x)<$\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the smallest and largest $y$-values in the graph.\n\n- The smallest $y$-value is $1.$ This occurs when $x=0.$\n\n- There is no largest $y$-value.\n\nThe function covers all the $y$-values between $1$ and $\\infty ,$ including $1.$\n\nTherefore, the range is $1\\le y<\\infty ,$ which can also be written as \n$1\\le f(x)<\\infty .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490222 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179651 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6df7104d-0f74-5301-9525-132c74c19ca9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Range of a Function by Considering Its End Behavior"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "077fd3b6-a66f-5992-89ff-f734285036e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1<f(x)<2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489533} {:answer/id #uuid "7bd4f26c-68d7-508b-ae68-df55769b27e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty <f(x)<1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489797} {:answer/id #uuid "85a2736a-d41e-5e05-9a88-0d47451e0a05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1<f(x)<\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489820} {:answer/id #uuid "a7ab8a0a-f5d1-5b79-8ad9-de72a82f973b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty <f(x)\\le 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489884} {:answer/id #uuid "c20f2f3c-997e-5379-8c75-90a1c8d674a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\le f(x)<\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489935}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7bd4f26c-68d7-508b-ae68-df55769b27e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty <f(x)<1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489797} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7dd7694-ccde-505f-9224-5e793348c4a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490197}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "24be045a-a1e0-562b-a001-f17a0f6f12b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-79997" :question/problem "![](images/ac/acbf4ae592e7912e6e27415ce99f872c4c0ec1737cfa3269635f37f7fa0fe5db.png)\n\nWhat is the range of the function $f(x),$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the smallest and largest $y$-values in the graph.\n\n- There is no smallest $y$-value.\n\n- The largest $y$-value is $1.$ This occurs when $x\\to -\\infty .$ However, note that this point is not included in the graph.\n\nThe function covers all the $y$-values between $-\\infty $ and $1,$ but not including $1.$\n\nTherefore, the range is $-\\infty <y<1,$ which can also be written as  $-\\infty <f(x)<1.$"}]}
